<!-- ================================================================= -->
<!--  Chapter 9: Understanding Variability                              -->
<!-- ================================================================= -->
<chapter xml:id="ch-variability" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Understanding Variability</title>

    <introduction xml:id="intro-ch9-variability">
        <p>
            Data is never perfectly consistent: measure the same thing twice and
            you will rarely get exactly the same answer.
            Track the population in a single country over fifty years and the
            numbers will shift, sometimes smoothly, sometimes in sudden jolts.
            This chapter is about variability: what it is, where it comes from,
            and how to measure it.
            In this chapter, we ....
            [TODO] We work through the main measures of variability (range,
            variance, standard deviation, interquartile range, and least
            squares) using life expectancy data from African countries as our
            running example.
            By the end of the chapter, you will be able to choose the right
            measure for a given situation, interpret what it tells you, and
            explain what it does not.
        </p>
    </introduction>

    <section xml:id="sec-variability-how-much">
        <title>How Much Does It Vary?</title>

        <introduction>
            <p>
                How long does it take to get somewhere in Nairobi?
                The honest answer is: it depends on where you are going, what
                time you leave, what day of the week it is, and a dozen other
                things you may not know until you are already in the traffic.
                In other words, journey times in any city vary.
                This chapter is about how to measure that variation.
            </p>

            <p>
                The
                <url href="https://www.tomtom.com/traffic-index/city/nairobi/">TomTom Traffic Index</url>
                records journey times across Nairobi continuously, aggregating
                anonymised GPS data from millions of trips.
                It found that in 2025 a 10 kilometre drive in the city took an
                average of 26 minutes and 47 seconds.
                But that single number conceals an enormous range.
                At 4am, the same 10 kilometres takes around 18 minutes.
                At 4pm on a weekday, it takes more than twice as long at around
                37 minutes.
            </p>

            <p>
                Over the entirety of 2025,
                <url href="https://www.tomtom.com/traffic-index/city/nairobi/">TomTom Traffic Index</url>
                estimated that the average Nairobi driver lost more than four
                and a half days (109 hours) sitting in traffic beyond what those
                same journeys would have taken at free-flow speeds.
            </p>

            <p>
                Survey data tells a similar story.
                A 2013 World Bank survey of 14,580 households across 15 Kenyan
                cities found that the median commute time in Nairobi was 30
                minutes.
                A separate survey from the same year found an average of 49
                minutes — more than 50 percent higher.
                Both surveys measured the same city in the same year.
                The difference reflects who was sampled, how questions were
                asked, and what counted as a commute.
                Matatu commuters spent roughly twice as long travelling as walk
                commuters making similar trips in the same city (Salon and
                Gulyani, 2019).
                And the variation is not unique to Nairobi: a study of Dar es
                Salaam found that a trip to the city centre could take anything
                from 30 minutes to three hours depending on traffic conditions
                (Andreasen and Møller-Jensen, 2017).
            </p>

            <p>
                None of these numbers contradict each other.
                They are all measuring the same underlying reality: journeys
                vary.
                A lot.
                The question this chapter asks is how to measure this variation.
                How do we summarise spread?
                What does a single number like 109 hours or a range of 18 to 37
                minutes actually tell us?
                And when two measures give different answers, how do we decide
                which one to use?
                These are the questions this chapter is about.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-traffic-variation-measuring">
            <title>Visualising Traffic Variation in Nairobi</title>

            <p>
                The heatmap below shows travel time across every hour of every
                day of the week in Nairobi, averaged across 2025.
                Each cell represents one two-hour slot on one day; darker red
                means longer journey times, lighter pink means shorter.
                The variation is immediately visible — some cells are nearly
                white, others deeply red.
                The total range from the lightest to the darkest cell captures,
                in one image, the same variation that the numbers above describe
                in words.
            </p>

            <figure xml:id="fig-tomtom-heatmap-week">
                <caption>
                    Travel time heatmap for Nairobi across all hours and days of
                    the week, averaged across 2025.
                    Darker red indicates longer journey times; lighter pink
                    indicates shorter.
                    Source: TomTom Traffic Index 2025,
                    <url href="https://www.tomtom.com/traffic-index/city/nairobi/" visual="tomtom.com/traffic-index/city/nairobi"/>.
                </caption>

                <image source="images/ch9_tomtom_heatmap_week.png" width="100%">
                    <description>
                        <p>
                            A heatmap with days of the week (Monday to Sunday)
                            across the top and hours of the day down the left
                            side.
                            Cells are coloured from light pink to dark red.
                            The darkest cells appear in the band from
                            approximately 5pm to 7pm on weekdays, particularly
                            Monday to Thursday.
                            Saturday and Sunday are substantially lighter
                            throughout, especially in the morning hours.
                            The 4am slot on Monday is notably light.
                        </p>
                    </description>
                </image>
            </figure>

            <p>
                The magnitude of that variation also differs across the city.
                The maps below show average traffic conditions during the
                morning and evening rush hours.
                The central business district and main arterial roads are
                consistently darker — more congested — while the outer suburbs
                are lighter.
                And the pattern shifts between morning and evening: congestion
                concentrates inward as commuters arrive in the morning, and
                spreads outward as they leave in the evening.
            </p>

            <figure xml:id="fig-tomtom-map-morning">
                <caption>
                    Traffic conditions in Nairobi during the morning rush hour,
                    averaged across 2025.
                    Yellow indicates moderate congestion, orange and red
                    indicate heavier delays.
                    Source: TomTom Traffic Index 2025.
                </caption>

                <image source="images/ch9_tomtom_map_morning.png" width="90%">
                    <description>
                        <p>
                            A hexagonal heatmap overlaid on a dark map of
                            Nairobi.
                            The hexagons covering the central business district
                            and major roads leading into it are coloured orange
                            to red, indicating significant congestion.
                            The outer suburbs and peripheral areas are
                            predominantly yellow, indicating lighter congestion.
                            The pattern shows congestion concentrated in the
                            city centre and along main arterial routes during
                            the morning peak.
                        </p>
                    </description>
                </image>
            </figure>

            <figure xml:id="fig-tomtom-map-evening">
                <caption>
                    Traffic conditions in Nairobi during the evening rush hour,
                    averaged across 2025.
                    Congestion spreads more broadly than in the morning,
                    extending further into residential areas as commuters make
                    their way home.
                    Source: TomTom Traffic Index 2025.
                </caption>

                <image source="images/ch9_tomtom_map_evening.png" width="90%">
                    <description>
                        <p>
                            A hexagonal heatmap overlaid on a dark map of
                            Nairobi during the evening rush hour.
                            Compared to the morning map, the orange and red
                            hexagons extend further outward from the CBD into
                            surrounding residential neighbourhoods.
                            The overall impression is of broader, more
                            distributed congestion than the morning peak,
                            consistent with commuters dispersing in multiple
                            directions rather than converging on a single
                            centre.
                        </p>
                    </description>
                </image>
            </figure>

            <p>
                These images show that variation exists and where it is largest.
                They do not yet tell us how to summarise it in a single number,
                or how to compare the spread in one dataset against another.
                That is what the rest of this chapter is for.
            </p>

            <p>
                <term>Sources:</term> TomTom Traffic Index 2025.
                Nairobi city profile.
                Available at:
                <url href="https://www.tomtom.com/traffic-index/city/nairobi/" visual="tomtom.com/traffic-index/city/nairobi"/>.
                Salon, D.
                and Gulyani, S.
                (2019).
                <q>Commuting in Urban Kenya: 
      Unpacking Travel Demand in Large and Small Kenyan Cities.</q>
                <em>Sustainability</em>, 11(14), 3823.
                <url href="https://doi.org/10.3390/su11143823"/>.
                CC BY 3.0.
                Andreasen, M.H.
                and Møller-Jensen, L.
                (2017).
                <q>Access to the 
      city: Mobility patterns, transport and accessibility in 
      peripheral settlements of Dar es Salaam.</q>
                <em>Journal of Transport Geography</em>, 62, 20 <ndash/> 29.
            </p>
        </subsection>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="sec-ch9-stack">
        <title>Variability STACK Question</title>

